            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s **Perseverance rover has completed a “marathon” on Mars**, reaching **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving distance. It hit the milestone on **Sol 1,890** of its mission, about **five years and four months** after landing—less than half the time it took NASA’s Opportunity rover to cover the same distance.【5:0†source】\n\nNASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured a new orbital image showing Perseverance as a tiny speck on the Martian surface, along with the rover’s long winding tracks. At the time, Perseverance was operating **west of Jezero Crater** in a region the science team nicknamed **“Arbot.”**【5:0†source】\n\nPerseverance remains active scientifically as well: it continues exploring beyond Jezero Crater, studying Mars’ geologic history, and collecting rock-core samples that could eventually be returned to Earth. NASA’s rover image archive is also still being updated, with recent “Image of the Week” entries for July 2026.【5:1†source】",
